 necessities of life to to  people but  it wouldn't really take a huge amount of  money  compared to what the gross national  income of the affluent countries  is to meet their basic needs  um so that we didn't have you know  millions or hundreds of millions of  people uh unable to get enough to eat or  living without decent sanitation or  not able to get basic health care we  could provide that for  maybe two or three percent of the gross  national product of the affluent world  and having done that we would still then  have funds to  fund the arts uh if if we wanted to and  i would hope that we would want to  uh once we'd met the more urgent needs  doesn't this kind of impro approach  imply that we should be focusing our  our attention on uh the consequences of  things like global inequality rather  than  dismantling the things that cause these  huge  inequalities doesn't capitalism corrupt  governments get a free pass with this  kind of approach of how to do good  well if you tell me you know your plan  for overthrowing capitalism and  replacing it with a better and fairer  economic system  i'm happy to look at that and if you  convince me that there's a real chance  that you're going to succeed in  in doing this then that might become a  very effective charity to donate to  but so far to continue watching this  video click the link in the top left or  in the description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

